1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,800
I built an optimizer for a 650,000 barrel-a-day refinery.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,800
At that scale, $1 per barrel of margin is about $237 million a year.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,800
The decision: which of five crudes, in what blend, at what cracking severity.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:22,500
This is the console. Five real published assays, EIA prices, quality specs enforced.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,500
First visit downloads Python in your browser — this starts after it loads.

6
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:35,600
At base prices the optimum is ~100% Alaska North Slope at maximum severity: $15.67 a barrel.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,400
Eleven percent above a naive equal-weight blend — and it matches the exact LP optimum to under half a cent.

8
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,800
Re-optimizing as prices move is worth another $2.56 a barrel.

9
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,600
Assays → TBP cut-point bridge → four product yields.

10
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:55,400
Extra Trees learn that physics; differential evolution searches it; an exact LP checks the answer.

11
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:59,800
Then 10,000 price scenarios — and 140 tests pass offline in CI.

12
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,600
Section 03: what it chose — and how often that choice changes.

13
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:12,000
Across 10,000 price worlds Alaska North Slope wins half the time; Forcados takes most of the rest.

14
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:17,800
So the diet isn't a fixed answer. It's a rule that depends on crack spreads.

15
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,400
Section 04 is the part most demos skip: risk.

16
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:28,000
Re-optimizing every scenario turns the worst 5% tail from −$3.50 to +$1.42.

17
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,800
Only 0.8% of price worlds lose money.

18
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,800
Flexibility isn't just margin. It's downside protection.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,800
And because the physics are affine, the answer is provable — not just plausible.

20
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,800
At the bottom, I set the baseline severity, then press Run.

21
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,600
This is a real click — the solver runs in the browser.

22
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:55,700
Differential evolution searches the diet and the severity, then checks itself against the exact LP.

23
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:00,200
DE and LP land on the same margin. That's the honest bar.

24
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,700
Solve time printed on screen — inside the 30-second budget.

25
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:13,100
ML surrogate, global optimization, live deployment — and the discipline to publish where it's weak.

26
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,200
Live demo: batestguy.github.io/dangote-refinery-optimizer

27
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,000
Code and full methodology: github.com/batestguy/dangote-refinery-optimizer

28
